1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,993
Slice an ice cream cone straight across.

2
00:00:02,993 --> 00:00:05,203
The cut edge is a perfect circle.

3
00:00:05,603 --> 00:00:08,535
Tilt the knife a little,
and you get an oval.

4
00:00:08,535 --> 00:00:11,175
Tilt it more, and the curve never closes.

5
00:00:11,175 --> 00:00:13,998
Cut straight down, and
you get another curve.

6
00:00:14,448 --> 00:00:16,247
Diya tried it in the kitchen today.

7
00:00:16,597 --> 00:00:18,687
One cone, four different curves.

8
00:00:18,687 --> 00:00:20,796
How can one shape hide all four?

9
00:00:21,296 --> 00:00:23,957
These are conic sections:
slices of a cone.

10
00:00:23,957 --> 00:00:26,386
Today, all four, and then the circle.

11
00:00:27,036 --> 00:00:28,762
First, a cone in maths.

12
00:00:28,762 --> 00:00:30,632
Stand one stick straight up.

13
00:00:30,632 --> 00:00:31,917
This is the axis.

14
00:00:32,317 --> 00:00:36,779
Cross a second stick through it at
a point V, at a fixed angle, alpha.

15
00:00:37,179 --> 00:00:41,236
Spin the second stick around the
axis, keeping alpha the same.

16
00:00:41,236 --> 00:00:42,815
It sweeps out a cone.

17
00:00:43,215 --> 00:00:45,569
In fact, two cones, tip to tip.

18
00:00:45,569 --> 00:00:50,230
V is the vertex, the spinning stick
is a generator, each cone a nappe.

19
00:00:50,880 --> 00:00:54,676
Now slice it with a flat
plane, like a sheet of glass.

20
00:00:54,676 --> 00:00:57,998
Beta is the angle between
the plane and the axis.

21
00:00:58,398 --> 00:01:01,123
At ninety degrees the
plane lies flat across.

22
00:01:01,123 --> 00:01:02,631
The slice is a circle.

23
00:01:03,031 --> 00:01:03,931
Tilt it.

24
00:01:03,763 --> 00:01:08,115
With beta between alpha and ninety
degrees, the slice is a closed oval,

25
00:01:08,115 --> 00:01:09,015
an ellipse.

26
00:01:09,389 --> 00:01:13,371
When beta equals alpha, the
plane runs along the side.

27
00:01:13,371 --> 00:01:15,161
The curve never closes.

28
00:01:15,161 --> 00:01:16,164
A parabola.

29
00:01:16,564 --> 00:01:20,623
When beta is smaller than alpha,
the plane cuts both cones.

30
00:01:20,623 --> 00:01:23,299
Two open curves, together a hyperbola.

31
00:01:23,949 --> 00:01:27,117
So one comparison decides
it: beta against alpha.

32
00:01:27,717 --> 00:01:28,617
Your turn.

33
00:01:28,486 --> 00:01:32,397
Keep beta at ninety degrees, but
push the plane through the vertex.

34
00:01:32,397 --> 00:01:33,627
What is the slice?

35
00:01:33,627 --> 00:01:34,759
Pause and decide.

36
00:01:37,959 --> 00:01:38,859
One point!

37
00:01:38,752 --> 00:01:40,621
The circle shrinks to nothing.

38
00:01:40,621 --> 00:01:45,261
Slices through the vertex give special
cases: a point, a line, or two crossing

39
00:01:45,261 --> 00:01:46,161
lines.

40
00:01:46,467 --> 00:01:47,579
Remember this.

41
00:01:47,579 --> 00:01:52,810
One cone, four slices: a circle, an
ellipse, a parabola and a hyperbola.

42
00:01:53,510 --> 00:01:55,641
Now the first slice, the circle.

43
00:01:55,991 --> 00:01:57,668
Turn a compass on paper.

44
00:01:57,668 --> 00:02:01,557
The pencil always stays the same
distance from the sharp point.

45
00:02:01,957 --> 00:02:03,769
That is the NCERT definition.

46
00:02:03,769 --> 00:02:07,856
A circle is the set of all points
in a plane at the same distance from

47
00:02:07,856 --> 00:02:08,970
one fixed point.

48
00:02:09,370 --> 00:02:11,650
The fixed point is the centre.

49
00:02:11,650 --> 00:02:14,039
The distance is the radius, r.

50
00:02:14,039 --> 00:02:17,151
As the point runs round, r never changes.

51
00:02:17,801 --> 00:02:19,063
Now the equation.

52
00:02:19,063 --> 00:02:24,004
Put the centre at h, k, and take
any point P, x, y, on the circle.

53
00:02:24,404 --> 00:02:25,304
Step one.

54
00:02:25,251 --> 00:02:27,647
Walk across from the centre to P.

55
00:02:27,647 --> 00:02:29,248
Across is x minus h.

56
00:02:29,998 --> 00:02:30,898
Step two.

57
00:02:30,853 --> 00:02:31,753
Walk up.

58
00:02:31,656 --> 00:02:33,063
Up is y minus k.

59
00:02:33,813 --> 00:02:34,713
Step three.

60
00:02:34,709 --> 00:02:37,911
Across, up and the radius
make a right triangle.

61
00:02:37,911 --> 00:02:41,901
By Pythagoras, across squared
plus up squared equals r squared.

62
00:02:42,651 --> 00:02:45,168
That is the equation of a circle.

63
00:02:45,168 --> 00:02:50,583
x minus h, all squared, plus y minus
k, all squared, equals r squared.

64
00:02:51,333 --> 00:02:54,805
If the centre is the
origin, h and k are zero.

65
00:02:54,805 --> 00:02:58,477
It becomes x squared plus
y squared equals r squared.

66
00:02:59,127 --> 00:03:00,199
Side by side.

67
00:03:00,199 --> 00:03:02,699
To read the centre, flip the signs.

68
00:03:02,699 --> 00:03:04,644
x minus two gives plus two.

69
00:03:04,644 --> 00:03:06,788
y plus three gives minus three.

70
00:03:07,188 --> 00:03:10,024
To read the radius, take a square root.

71
00:03:10,024 --> 00:03:14,064
Sixteen is r squared, so the
radius is four, not sixteen.

72
00:03:14,664 --> 00:03:15,577
Your turn.

73
00:03:15,577 --> 00:03:19,514
x squared, plus y minus five
all squared, equals nine.

74
00:03:19,514 --> 00:03:20,910
Centre and radius?

75
00:03:20,910 --> 00:03:22,254
Pause and decide.

76
00:03:25,454 --> 00:03:27,834
Centre zero, five, radius three!

77
00:03:27,834 --> 00:03:32,423
x squared is x minus zero, all
squared, and three squared is nine.

78
00:03:33,073 --> 00:03:34,763
Now, one worked example.

79
00:03:35,113 --> 00:03:40,445
Find the centre and radius of x squared
plus y squared, minus six x, plus

80
00:03:40,445 --> 00:03:43,233
four y, minus twelve, equals zero.

81
00:03:43,733 --> 00:03:44,633
Step one.

82
00:03:44,511 --> 00:03:48,763
Group the x terms, group the y
terms, and move the twelve across.

83
00:03:49,513 --> 00:03:50,413
Step two.

84
00:03:50,293 --> 00:03:51,700
Complete the squares.

85
00:03:51,700 --> 00:03:54,591
Half of minus six is
minus three: add nine.

86
00:03:54,591 --> 00:03:56,750
Half of four is two: add four.

87
00:03:56,750 --> 00:03:58,319
Add both on each side.

88
00:03:59,069 --> 00:03:59,991
Step three.

89
00:03:59,991 --> 00:04:04,740
That gives x minus three all squared,
plus y plus two all squared, equals

90
00:04:04,740 --> 00:04:05,712
twenty five.

91
00:04:06,462 --> 00:04:10,321
So the centre is three, minus
two, and the radius is five.

92
00:04:10,971 --> 00:04:12,434
Now, the exam trap.

93
00:04:12,784 --> 00:04:16,400
x squared minus four x,
plus y squared, equals zero.

94
00:04:16,400 --> 00:04:20,699
Many students add four on the
left only, and say it is a point.

95
00:04:21,099 --> 00:04:24,768
But what you add on the
left, you add on the right.

96
00:04:24,768 --> 00:04:26,491
Zero plus four is four.

97
00:04:26,491 --> 00:04:29,765
A real circle: centre
two, zero, radius two.

98
00:04:30,415 --> 00:04:31,807
Where is this asked?

99
00:04:31,807 --> 00:04:32,769
In JEE Main.

100
00:04:32,769 --> 00:04:37,039
In January twenty twenty four, a
question began with a circle's equation,

101
00:04:37,039 --> 00:04:39,730
and you first needed
its centre and radius.

102
00:04:40,330 --> 00:04:41,489
Exam level now.

103
00:04:41,489 --> 00:04:45,732
A circle touches both axes and
passes through the point two, one.

104
00:04:45,732 --> 00:04:48,571
What is the sum of all its possible radii?

105
00:04:48,571 --> 00:04:49,779
Pause and solve.

106
00:04:55,179 --> 00:04:56,745
The answer is C.

107
00:04:56,745 --> 00:04:57,645
Six.

108
00:04:57,864 --> 00:05:00,692
Two, one is in the top right quarter.

109
00:05:00,692 --> 00:05:05,546
A circle there touching both axes is
r away from each, so its centre is

110
00:05:05,546 --> 00:05:06,446
r, r.

111
00:05:06,806 --> 00:05:08,878
It passes through two, one.

112
00:05:08,878 --> 00:05:14,161
So two minus r all squared, plus one
minus r all squared, equals r squared.

113
00:05:14,661 --> 00:05:18,895
That tidies up to r squared minus
six r plus five equals zero.

114
00:05:18,895 --> 00:05:20,546
So r is one or five.

115
00:05:20,546 --> 00:05:21,818
The sum is six.

116
00:05:22,418 --> 00:05:23,958
Here is your recap.

117
00:05:23,958 --> 00:05:25,541
Screenshot this card.

118
00:05:28,741 --> 00:05:30,927
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

119
00:05:31,327 --> 00:05:32,227
One.

120
00:05:31,746 --> 00:05:33,948
Which slice of a cone gives a parabola?

121
00:05:34,848 --> 00:05:35,748
Two.

122
00:05:35,353 --> 00:05:40,252
Find the centre and radius of x plus
one all squared, plus y squared, equals

123
00:05:40,252 --> 00:05:41,163
forty nine.

124
00:05:42,063 --> 00:05:42,963
Three.

125
00:05:42,622 --> 00:05:47,091
Write the equation of the circle with
centre two, zero, and radius three.

126
00:05:48,191 --> 00:05:51,954
Next time: a cricket ball's flight
and a dish antenna share one secret

127
00:05:51,954 --> 00:05:52,854
curve.

128
00:05:52,479 --> 00:05:53,379
Why?

129
00:05:52,917 --> 00:05:53,817
The parabola.
